# Hourly Patterns

The Hourly Patterns report answers the question "which hours of the day go
well, and which are harder?". It lays every day of the period over one 24-hour
clock and compares the hours.

<Video id="hourly-stats" />

## What it shows

In this report, in range means a reading from 70 to 180 mg/dL. Below range is
under 70 mg/dL, and very low is under 54 mg/dL. The tight range, 70 to
140 mg/dL, is a narrower part of the target. The app shows these values in
mmol/L if you chose mmol/L.

### At a glance

Three columns name the hours that stand out:

- **Most time in range**: the hours with the largest share of readings in
  range.
- **Least time in range**: the hours with the smallest share of readings in
  range. Each one says which way the hour went: below range, above range, or
  both.
- **Most time below range**: the hours with the largest share of readings
  under 70 mg/dL. Only hours that went below range on at least 2 days are in
  this list.

Each hour has a small bar that shows how its readings divide across the ranges.

### Hour by hour

Each bar is one hour of the day. The bands in the bar show the share of that
hour's readings in each range. The lowest range is at the bottom.

### Every hour

The table gives the same figures for all 24 hours: **In range**, **Below** and
**Above**, the **Median** glucose, and the **Middle half**. The middle half is
the range that holds the central 50% of the readings in that hour. The
**Readings** and **Days** columns show how much data each hour has.

## How to use it

The report opens on the last 14 days. Two weeks can hide the real pattern. To
use more data:

1. At the top of the report, select the date range.
2. Select **30 Days**.
3. Select **Apply Filters**. The report works out each hour again from 30 days
   of data.
4. Look at **At a glance** again. Make sure the same hours stand out.

To see the full daily curve, select **See the full daily curve in the Glucose
Profile** at the bottom of the page.

## Good to know

- **Hours with too little data.** The report compares an hour with the others
  only when the hour has readings on at least 5 different days, and at least 20
  readings in all. An hour with less data shows **Too little data** in the
  table. It still shows in the chart, but not in **At a glance**.
- **Close results.** The report names a best or worst hour only when the hours
  differ by at least 5 percentage points of time in range. If all hours are
  closer than that, **At a glance** says that no hour stands out.
- **Time zone.** The hours follow the time zone in your profile settings. If no
  time zone is set, or a share link cannot read it, the report uses the local
  time that each device recorded. The **How to read this report** card at the
  bottom says which one the report uses.

<Callout type="info" title="A pattern, not a reason">

  This report shows what happened at each hour. It does not show why. Meals,
  activity, sleep, illness and sensor problems can all change an hour. Discuss
  a pattern with your diabetes care team before you change your treatment.

</Callout>
